descriptions.value:
When it denormalizes data into a class, the Serializer computes which attributes it may write from the #[Ignore] and #[Groups] metadata. It did that for the requested class, but wrote into another class in two cases. The first case is the class that a discriminator map picks from the data. The second is the class of the object passed with the object_to_populate or deep_object_to_populate option.

When the requested class has no #[Ignore] attribute and the context sets no groups, nothing was restricted. A client could then set a property that the picked class marks with #[Ignore], constructor arguments included:

#[DiscriminatorMap(typeProperty: 'kind', mapping: ['email' => EmailNotification::class])]
abstract class Notification {}

class EmailNotification extends Notification
{
    #[Ignore]
    public bool $skipModeration = false;
}

With a #[MapRequestPayload] Notification $notification argument, the payload {"kind":"email","skipModeration":true} set skipModeration. With groups in the context, a property outside these groups could be set too. This happened when another class of the map had a property with the same name in one of the groups. The same happened with #[Ignore] when the requested class declares the map itself. On 5.4, ObjectNormalizer also returned such properties when normalizing an object whose class declares the map itself.

This affects ObjectNormalizer, PropertyNormalizer and GetSetMethodNormalizer.

The Serializer now resolves the class it writes into first, and computes the allowed attributes for that class. The type property of a discriminator map is also written only when the metadata of that class allows it.

In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

module: Fix kernel panic when a symbol st_shndx is out of bounds

The module loader doesn't check for bounds of the ELF section index in
simplify_symbols():

       for (i = 1; i < symsec->sh_size / sizeof(Elf_Sym); i++) {
		const char *name = info->strtab + sym[i].st_name;

		switch (sym[i].st_shndx) {
		case SHN_COMMON:

		[...]

		default:
			/* Divert to percpu allocation if a percpu var. */
			if (sym[i].st_shndx == info->index.pcpu)
				secbase = (unsigned long)mod_percpu(mod);
			else
  /** HERE --> **/		secbase = info->sechdrs[sym[i].st_shndx].sh_addr;
			sym[i].st_value += secbase;
			break;
		}
	}

A symbol with an out-of-bounds st_shndx value, for example 0xffff
(known as SHN_XINDEX or SHN_HIRESERVE), may cause a kernel panic:

  BUG: unable to handle page fault for address: ...
  RIP: 0010:simplify_symbols+0x2b2/0x480
  ...
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception

This can happen when module ELF is legitimately using SHN_XINDEX or
when it is corrupted.

Add a bounds check in simplify_symbols() to validate that st_shndx is
within the valid range before using it.

This issue was discovered due to a bug in llvm-objcopy, see relevant
discussion for details [1].

[1] https://lore.kernel.org/linux-modules/20251224005752.201911-1-ihor.solodrai@linux.dev/

In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

KVM: SEV: Drop WARN on large size for KVM_MEMORY_ENCRYPT_REG_REGION

Drop the WARN in sev_pin_memory() on npages overflowing an int, as the
WARN is comically trivially to trigger from userspace, e.g. by doing:

  struct kvm_enc_region range = {
          .addr = 0,
          .size = -1ul,
  };

  __vm_ioctl(vm, KVM_MEMORY_ENCRYPT_REG_REGION, &range);

Note, the checks in sev_mem_enc_register_region() that presumably exist to
verify the incoming address+size are completely worthless, as both "addr"
and "size" are u64s and SEV is 64-bit only, i.e. they _can't_ be greater
than ULONG_MAX.  That wart will be cleaned up in the near future.

	if (range->addr > ULONG_MAX || range->size > ULONG_MAX)
		return -EINVAL;

Opportunistically add a comment to explain why the code calculates the
number of pages the "hard" way, e.g. instead of just shifting @ulen.

In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

tracing: Fix potential deadlock in cpu hotplug with osnoise

The following sequence may leads deadlock in cpu hotplug:

    task1        task2        task3
    -----        -----        -----

 mutex_lock(&interface_lock)

            [CPU GOING OFFLINE]

            cpus_write_lock();
            osnoise_cpu_die();
              kthread_stop(task3);
                wait_for_completion();

                      osnoise_sleep();
                        mutex_lock(&interface_lock);

 cpus_read_lock();

 [DEAD LOCK]

Fix by swap the order of cpus_read_lock() and mutex_lock(&interface_lock).

workarounds.value:
Disable the ssl_preread directive in favor of SSL/TLS termination on NGINX. For more information, refer to  https://nginx.org/en/docs/stream/ngx_stream_ssl_preread_module.html 

For example, the following configuration includes the ssl_preread on; directive:



stream {
    log_format sni "PREREAD_SNI=[$ssl_preread_server_name] -> [$backend]";
    access_log /var/log/nginx/stream.log sni;
    map $ssl_preread_server_name $backend {
        internal.test   127.0.0.1:9002;   # privileged backend
        default           127.0.0.1:9001;   # public backend
    }
    server {
        listen 9443;
        ssl_preread on;
        proxy_pass $backend;
    }
}

To mitigate the issue for this configuration, make the following changes:

  *  Remove the ssl_preread directive.
  *  Terminate TLS on NGINX by adding the following:  *  Change the listener to ssl.
  *  Configure a certificate and key.
  *  Replacing $ssl_preread_server_name with $ssl_server_name to use SNI-based routing.





stream {
    log_format sni "TLS_SNI=[$ssl_server_name] -> [$backend]";
    access_log /var/log/nginx/stream.log sni;
    map $ssl_server_name $backend {
        internal.test   127.0.0.1:9002;   # privileged backend
        default           127.0.0.1:9001;   # public backend
    }
    server {
        listen 9443 ssl;
        ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/ssl/fullchain.pem;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/privkey.pem;
        ssl_protocols       TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
        # ssl_preread removed; NGINX terminates client TLS.
        proxy_pass $backend;
        # Use these if the backends still expect TLS.
        proxy_ssl on;
        proxy_ssl_server_name on;
        proxy_ssl_name $ssl_server_name;
    }
}

After making the configuration changes, validate the configuration and reload, using the following commands:

nginx -t
nginx -s reload

